  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday... Ridging will be the main force to\nstart this week. This will favor continued above normal\ntemperatures with highs in the 50s. This will be supported by\nsoutherly flow not just at the surface but aloft as well\nproviding for WAA. These large scale features are driving the\nwarmer temperatures. This will also promote mixing in the lower \natmosphere as we warm during the daytime. Therefore gusty winds \nare expected with a peak in the afternoon along a typical \ndiurnal trend. After this morning, overnight lows will be above \nfreezing as well. No chance for precipitation this period due to\na lack of any forcing mechanism.\n\nThursday into next weekend... Thursday will see the arrival of a\nshortwave passing to our north. With moisture lacking the main \nrain chances will be closer to the strongest lift to our north. \nRain chances on Thursday have gone down each day as we have \ngotten closer. Chances are not zero, but are low (<20% chance). \nThis shortwave will also allow for some cooler air to move in \nacross parts of central Minnesota. In these cooler areas highs \nwill top out in the 40s, but across the rest of the area 50s \nare again expected. On Thursday a larger wave will be present \nacross the western CONUS and this wave will move over us on \nSaturday. In the guidance the low track continues to vary, but \nmodel trends currently favor the main surface low passing to \nour north. This will keep us in the warm sector and \nprecipitation type for the main phase of this system will be \nrain. NBM PoPs remain high and confidence remains high based on \nthe global ensembles. The variability remains in QPF, but \noverall the ensemble mean and deterministic values favor the \nhighest QPF to our north closer to the surface low. The warmer \ntemperatures will continue until we move into colder airmass on \nthe back side of the system late Saturday into Sunday. This will\nbring back lows below freezing and highs below normal in mid to\nupper 30s. These cooler temperatures would support snow as the\nprimary precipitation type. The question is will enough lift \nand moisture remain once this colder air arrives? Some members \nof the ENS and GEPS (European and Canadian ensemble systems) \nshow that parts of central Minnesota could have enough omega and\nsaturation to see some snow fall. So not impossible, but not yet\nthe most likely solution. Looking ahead, colder temperatures \nlook to hold through the Thanksgiving holiday.",
